    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49555.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, either way, it's going to be. I mean, I'm not. I mean, if the recess appointment, okay, fine. But to me, there always has to be a vote. There's a vote to go to allow a recess to take place when, and if you have that vote knowing there's going to be appointments made during that recess, then the vote to go to the recess is in fact almost a vote on the actual nominees. So I think you should just follow the process, follow the rules. The president wins. He's got a mandate. The president nominates who he wants. We should be in favor of putting in who he wants. But we have in our Constitution a very important feature which says the Senate does the advise and consent and actually confirms those nominees. I say let the process work and there's no way to avoid both. There are going to be a vote to allow this process to move forward in a typical fashion, traditional fashion or in this recess. If it goes for the recent appointment, there's a vote. And everyone's going to know how people voted and what that vote was about. So you can avoid that. And frankly, we shouldn't want to avoid that in a constitutional democracy constitutional republic like we have in America. So let's follow the process, follow the rules again. As I said, follow the rules when it comes to the after sport, follow the rules when it comes to the nomination and confirmation process. That works for us, the greatest country ever. Let's let that process play out. but i think",
